n as  you guys could see previously it was way  too stiff with a two and one  it starts out easily and then it  actually gets much stiffer and then you  can't finish opening it you have to use  it with two hands and you would think  that maybe that's done just for the  touchscreen so to keep it more stable  but this one is actually more stable  even though it's easier to open if I  shake my table here  see that the two-in-one screen actually  moves around more now before we talk  about keyboards and trackpad let me show  you some other differences externally  let's flip these two over there's  definitely some differences in cooling  and design so at the top here we have  these vertical slots compared to these  horizontal ones here and then on the  sides the two and one actually has three  smaller ones compared to two longer ones  now the bottom ones are used for  speakers we'll talk about that in just a  bit there are some differences top one  is used to suck in air flow the new XPS  it actually has a little bit more curved  sort of like a MacBook over here so that  when you put them on your lap there is  less chances of these vents and the  speaker's being blocked it has a little  bit better access to air flow which is  great now the ports are absolutely  identical we have two Thunderbolt three  ports both full-speed that's also used  for charging and we have the micro SD  card slot over here and then a headphone  jack now the shape of the track pads are  slightly different but as far as the  feel and the quality they are basically  the same now as far as the keyboards  you'll see that we have page up and page  down on the sides whereas the new XPS it  actually builds that into the the up and  down key so you have to hold a function  button now you might be able to also  notice that this keyboard is actually  recessed a little bit more so than the  two-in-one and that is because the new  XPS 13 for 2020 has more key travel  actually has a little bit more feedback  